From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bpereira <bpereira@cloudflare.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 23:35:26 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Gate per-resolve work on damage

Four passes in resolve re-derived their output from scratch every frame,
each one a pure function of state that mostly had not changed.

Image layers. flush_styles_to_layout re-derived every node's background
and mask image layers from its computed style, including the
overwhelming majority whose style did not change; an undamaged node
already holds the right layers. 28us of a 315us resolve on a heavy page.

Taffy styles. to_taffy_style is the most expensive thing the flush walk
does, and the gate that skips it already existed here, commented out,
guarding exactly the damage bits that mean "this node's layout style
changed". Damage alone is not sufficient, though: stylo_taffy stores a
calc() length as a raw pointer into the ComputedValues it converted, so
the taffy style borrows from the cascade with nothing tracking it, and a
paint-only restyle (a running colour transition, say) reports neither
bit while still replacing and freeing those ComputedValues. Record which
ones a style came from and rebuild whenever they change. A typical
heavy-page resolve goes 245us -> 158us, and the pointer check does not
move it. Kept off the non-incremental path, which has no damage to
consult.

Tree walks. On a 271k-node page with ten damaged nodes, propagate_damage
visited every node, resolve_layout_children made 42.6k visits and opened
its construct gate zero times, and compute_rehomed_reach ran two
full-slab scans for hosts that usually do not exist.
NodeFlags::HAS_DAMAGED_SUBTREE is stamped from each damaged node up to
the root during accumulate_damage_summary, which already reads every
node's damage, and both walks return early on a clean subtree. Two
classes must be walked even when clean, so they are marked explicitly:
hoist owners, which have to re-offer a remembered box or it attaches to
nothing once the target re-collects, and nodes created during the walk
itself, which cannot have been marked and are caught by the existing "no
cached children" rule. compute_rehomed_reach tracks its hosts at the
single push site and remembers the chains it wrote, so it clears and
rescans only those. Damage 6.1ms -> 0.065ms, construct 2.9ms -> 0.13ms,
rehomed reach 2.2ms -> 41ns.

Counters. counters_seen is a sticky positive (one list item anywhere
turns it on for good, and most navigation menus trip it) after which
resolve_counters rebuilt the whole tree every resolve. Output is a
function of tree shape and the counter-affecting styles alone, so gate
on CONSTRUCT damage: structural mutations stamp ALL_DAMAGE, and display
/ list-style / content / counter-* diffs all rebuild the box tree, so
every input that can move a counter is covered while plain RELAYOUT
damage cannot. On a 65k-node document that took counters from 8.3ms to
0.95ms. Answering the gate still meant a slab scan to prove a negative
on paint-only frames, so accumulate_damage_summary now records whether
any damage was CONSTRUCT damage and moves ahead of resolve_counters, and
the gate reads the flag: 2.1ms -> 41ns.

Ordering is what makes that last fusion safe. Exactly one thing raises
damage between the summary and where the gate used to run:
damage_moved_counters, which stamps CONSTRUCT_BOX on the counters it saw
move. That set is explicit rather than discovered by scanning, so it is
handed to note_late_layout_damage, which folds it into the summary and
the damaged marking. Dropping it fails
renumber_reaches_an_undamaged_subtree: inserting into one list renumbers
a sibling list that carries no damage of its own, and the construct walk
would never reach it.

A damage write stages its node in the same call. Both the summary and
the end-of-resolve clear walk the bitset, so a write that does not stage
is invisible to each of them and leaves the node dirty but skipped; and
nothing in a release build reports it. Two accessors own that pairing,
one for a write through existing ElementData and one for a first init,
since ensure_init stamps ALL_DAMAGE itself; every writer in mutator,
construct and damage goes through them, and stylo stages from its own
&self handles. The debug-only full-slab cross-check is only a partial
backstop: dropping the init-path stage fails five tests, dropping the
other one fails none, because stylo's traversal re-stages those nodes
anyway.

Flush is gated too, which the first cut of this commit said was
impossible. The obstacle was real: flush must rebuild a taffy style
whenever stylo reallocates ComputedValues, which happens with no damage
at all, so damage alone is the wrong signal. Recording the
ComputedValues a style was built from turns that into a pointer compare,
and a node stylo never took a handle to cannot have moved. On top of
that, a subtree with no damage under it, no box that moved and nothing
scrolled re-offers the stacking-context contribution it made last
resolve instead of rebuilding it. Paint-only flush 11.0ms -> 0.4ms,
whole resolve 11.9ms -> 1.3ms, reflow unchanged. Debug builds take the
full path and assert the cached contribution matches a recompute.

Skipping round_layout is what forced the last piece. Sticky, the fixed
re-anchor and the relative %-inset fixup all moved a box by writing
final_layout.location, and all three assumed taffy had just wiped it:
one re-applied its shift with no ledger at all, so a skipped rounding
pass accumulated it and a fixed navbar drifted further down on every
scroll. Node::pin_offset records what is baked
into a location and is zeroed by set_final_layout, the single place a
location is written wholesale, so the passes now state their shift
absolutely and reconcile the delta. That deletes the sticky ledger, the
fixed anchor ledger and the reset flag they were driven by.

Against blitz-dom 0.3.0-beta.3: kept the counters gate (CONSTRUCT damage
recorded by the flat summary scan, which now runs before
resolve_counters; note_late_layout_damage; counter-* diffs rebuild the
box), the rehomed_reach host list and touched chains (hosts pushed where
paint_tree.rs records a re-home), the round_layout / inline-break
restore skip, Node::pin_offset / set_pin_offset (the embedder's sticky
pass) and note_boxes_moved, and a clean-subtree early return in
resolve_layout_children keyed on upstream's damaged_descendants flag.
note_boxes_moved now bumps the geometry generation, which is what
memoised paint positions key on.

Dropped as absorbed or moot: the damage staging bitset and
HAS_DAMAGED_SUBTREE (upstream keeps the damaged-descendants invariant
and gates propagate_damage_flags and the end-of-resolve clear on it;
that clear walk now also enters shadow trees, which it used to reach
through the bitset), the taffy-style / calc-pointer gating and
image-layer gating (no flush_styles_to_layout; taffy styles are built on
the fly and style images flush from a queue), the cached
stacking-context contributions and hoist_cache_len, hoist owners and the
fixed-anchor ledger (native taffy out-of-flow layout, no hoisting, no
layout-location bake). The content: attr() dirtiness tracking for the
counters gate is dropped too: stylo 0.22 substitutes attr() at
computed-value time, so an attribute write restyles the pseudo and its
content diff rebuilds the box.
